WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.135 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Could the same pencil seem longer just because someone moved
the ruler?

00:00:04.785 --> 00:00:08.094 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Imagine two friends measuring it from different marks.

00:00:08.314 --> 00:00:13.392 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Before we trust a number, let us investigate the unit, the
scale and the starting point.

00:00:14.042 --> 00:00:18.664 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to measure length accurately using
useful metric units.

00:00:19.314 --> 00:00:21.071 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Success criteria.

00:00:21.291 --> 00:00:24.243 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can choose a sensible unit and instrument.

00:00:24.463 --> 00:00:27.422 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can read a ruler from the correct starting point.

00:00:27.642 --> 00:00:31.001 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can combine metres and centimetres accurately.

00:00:31.651 --> 00:00:34.619 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Remember that a measure counts equal units.

00:00:34.839 --> 00:00:41.656 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Millimetres help with small details, centimetres suit a
pencil, metres suit a room, and kilometres suit a long
journey.

00:00:41.876 --> 00:00:44.312 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose a tool with useful markings.

00:00:44.532 --> 00:00:49.211 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Smaller units can give a more precise measurement, rather
than changing the object.

00:00:49.861 --> 00:00:54.517 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A ribbon begins at the two centimetre mark and ends at nine
centimetres.

00:00:54.737 --> 00:00:56.308 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Predict its length.

00:00:56.528 --> 00:01:02.436 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Count the equal one-centimetre intervals between those
marks, rather than reading the final label alone.

00:01:03.086 --> 00:01:05.721 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
There are seven centimetres between the marks.

00:01:05.941 --> 00:01:08.344 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Nine minus two equals seven.

00:01:08.564 --> 00:01:15.402 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Moving the ruler does not stretch the ribbon: subtract the
starting reading whenever the object is not lined up with
zero.

00:01:16.052 --> 00:01:20.108 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A display board measures two metres and thirty-five
centimetres.

00:01:20.328 --> 00:01:22.890 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We want a single centimetre reading.

00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:28.829 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
One metre contains one hundred centimetres, so the two
metres account for two hundred centimetres.

00:01:29.479 --> 00:01:32.291 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Add the remaining thirty-five centimetres.

00:01:32.511 --> 00:01:36.700 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Two hundred plus thirty-five makes two hundred and
thirty-five centimetres.

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:41.737 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The combined measure and the single-unit measure describe
exactly the same length.

00:01:42.387 --> 00:01:46.652 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Reading nine centimetres as the ribbon length ignores where
it started.

00:01:46.872 --> 00:01:48.924 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Count the space between the marks.

00:01:49.144 --> 00:01:53.439 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A different starting mark changes the subtraction, but not
the physical ribbon.

00:01:54.089 --> 00:01:58.569 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A strip starts at three centimetres and ends at eleven
centimetres.

00:01:58.789 --> 00:02:00.203 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
How long is it?

00:02:04.203 --> 00:02:07.218 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Eleven minus three is eight centimetres.

00:02:07.438 --> 00:02:13.146 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Check by counting eight equal intervals, or placing a
zero-start ruler beside the same strip.

00:02:13.796 --> 00:02:18.116 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose a useful unit, read the interval size and check the
starting mark.

00:02:18.336 --> 00:02:23.195 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
For a practical investigation, measure a pencil in
centimetres and millimetres.

00:02:23.415 --> 00:02:27.099 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Discuss which reading describes its length more precisely.

00:02:27.319 --> 00:02:30.144 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Reflect on which model helped you and why.

00:02:30.364 --> 00:02:34.804 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Now try the MathMagic practice, and explain your thinking to
someone.
